INT. FARMHOUSE BASEMENT - NIGHT

Dirt floor. A single bare bulb on a cord. Shelves of mason jars. A furnace thumps like a heartbeat.

DESMOND "DEZ" KOWALCZYK, thirty, and his brother CALLUM KOWALCZYK, twenty-eight, crouch by a low iron door in the far wall. Dez holds a crowbar, Callum a flashlight. Their cousin RHONDA, thirty-five, stands on the steps, arms wrapped round herself.

RHONDA
Dez, don't.

DEZ
We have to know.

CALLUM
Dez is right.

RHONDA
Callum, you're not helping.

CALLUM
Rhonda, I'm trying.

He aims the light at the iron door. It is covered in scratches, long and deep. Dez sets the crowbar in the seam and heaves. Nothing.

DEZ
It's rusted shut.

CALLUM
Together.

They push. The door groans. Rhonda gasps. Something inside gives a long low hiss, and the bulb flickers, then dies. The furnace stops thumping.

RHONDA
(in the dark)
Dez? Callum?

DEZ
I'm here.

CALLUM
I'm here.

RHONDA
Something's breathing.

DEZ
That's us.

RHONDA
No. It isn't.

The bulb flares back. Dez and Callum are alone on the dirt floor. The door is wide open. The steps are empty. Rhonda's voice calls from the dark behind the iron, small and cheerful.

RHONDA (O.S.)
Come on in, boys!
